      "body": "## Context\n\n`repairBrokenSubmodules()` in `cmd/gitops/init.go` reads `.gitmodules` and re-registers any submodules that were committed as regular trees (040000) instead of gitlinks (160000 commit). It has no unit test coverage.\n\n## What to test\n\nAdd tests in `cmd/gitops/init_test.go` covering:\n\n1. **Broken submodule repaired** — create a git repo with a `.gitmodules` entry and the extension committed as a regular tree (not a submodule). Verify it gets re-registered as a proper submodule (160000 commit in `git ls-tree`).\n2. **Healthy submodule unchanged** — create a repo with a properly registered submodule. Verify it returns 0 repaired.\n3. **No .gitmodules file** — verify 0 repaired and no error.\n4. **Empty .gitmodules** — verify 0 repaired and no error.\n5. **Broken gitlink file removed** — create a `.git` file (gitlink) in the extension dir. Verify it is cleaned up during repair.\n\n## Hints\n\n- Use `t.TempDir()` for test directories\n- To create the broken state: `git init`, add files, write `.gitmodules` manually, `git add -A`, `git commit` — this commits extensions as trees\n- To verify repair: run `git ls-tree HEAD extensions/` and check for `160000` prefix\n- The function signature is `repairBrokenSubmodules(installPath string) (int, error)`\n- These tests need network access to clone from the submodule URL in `.gitmodules`\n\nPart of #241",
