      "body": "## Context\n\n`convertToSubmodules()` in `cmd/gitops/init.go` scans a directory for standalone git repos and converts them to git submodules, preserving the checked-out commit. It has no unit test coverage.\n\n## What to test\n\nAdd table-driven tests in `cmd/gitops/init_test.go` covering:\n\n1. **Standalone repo converted** — create a temp git repo with a subdirectory containing a cloned git repo (with a remote). Verify it gets converted to a submodule.\n2. **Commit preserved** — verify the submodule is checked out at the same commit as before conversion.\n3. **No remote skipped** — create a subdirectory with a `.git` directory but no remote. Verify it is skipped.\n4. **Non-git directory skipped** — create a subdirectory with no `.git` at all. Verify it is left as-is.\n5. **Gitlink file skipped** — create a subdirectory with a `.git` file (not directory). Verify it is skipped.\n6. **Empty directory** — verify no error when the scanned directory is empty.\n7. **Missing directory** — verify no error when the scanned directory doesn't exist.\n\n## Hints\n\n- Use `t.TempDir()` for test directories\n- Use `os.Exec` or `execute.Run` to set up real git repos in temp dirs (init, add remote, commit)\n- Follow the existing table-driven test pattern in `init_test.go`\n- The function signature is `convertToSubmodules(installPath, dirName string) error`\n\nPart of #241",
